far based on the targets of your task you will fly relative to the minimum and maximum possible tasks. -100% indicates the minimum AAT distance. 0% is the nominal AAT distance. +100% is the maximum AAT distance.␀Speed remaining␀Achieved MacCready␀␀Achieved speed␀␀Cruise efficiency␀␀␀Efficiency of cruise. 100 indicates perfect MacCready performance; greater than 100 indicates better than MacCready performance is achieved through flying in streets. Less than 100 is appropriate if you fly considerably off-track. This value estimates your cruise efficiency according to the current flight history with the set MC value. Calculation begins after task is started.␀Valid start␀Start time␀␀Start alt.␀␀Start speed␀Finish min. alt.␀␀␀␀Valid finish␀␀␀␀â€”␀Local time␀␀UTC time␀␀␀␀UTC date␀␀␀␀Flight time␀Takeoff time␀␀␀␀Landing time␀␀␀␀Daylight time␀␀␀%04d-%02d-%02d␀␀%s (%d/%d)␀␀magnify␀shrink␀␀Sim: Jump to␀␀␀␀.cupx␀␀␀GoTo␀␀␀␀Pan To␀␀wptimg␀␀Failed to load %s: %s␀␀␀Waypoint Editor␀Save changes to waypoint file?␀␀Waypoints edited␀␀␀␀Delete waypoint?␀␀␀␀Short Name␀␀Sorry, the waypoint editor is not yet available for the UTM coordinate format.␀␀Landable␀␀␀␀Mountain Pass␀␀␀Mountain Top␀␀␀␀Transmitter Mast␀␀␀␀Tower␀␀␀Tunnel␀␀Bridge␀␀Power Plant␀Dam␀Castle␀␀Intersection␀␀␀␀Marker␀␀Control Point␀␀␀PG Take Off␀PG Landing Zone␀Choose a filter or click here␀␀␀No Match!␀␀␀HDG(%s)␀Direction␀␀␀Select Waypoint␀Left FAI Triangle␀␀␀Right FAI Triangle␀␀Map file␀␀␀␀Recently Used␀␀␀Thermal hotspot␀Colours␀Filter␀␀Airspace display␀␀␀␀Controls filtering of airspace for display and warnings. The airspace filter button also allows filtering of display and warnings independently for each airspace class.␀␀␀␀Clip altitude␀␀␀For clip airspace mode, this is the altitude below which airspace is displayed.␀Margin␀␀For auto and all below airspace mode, this is the altitude above/below which airspace is included.␀␀Label visibility␀␀␀␀Determines what labels are displayed.␀␀␀Enable/disable all airspace warnings.␀␀␀Warnings dialog␀Enable/disable displaying airspaces warnings dialog.␀␀␀␀Warning time␀␀␀␀This is the time before an airspace incursion is estimated at which the system will warn the pilot.␀Repetitive sound␀␀␀␀Enable/disable repetitive warning sound when airspaces warnings dialog is displayed.␀␀␀␀Acknowledge time␀␀␀␀This is the time period in which an acknowledged airspace warning will not be repeated.␀Use black outline␀␀␀Draw a black outline around each airspace rather than the airspace color.␀␀␀Airspace fill mode␀␀Specifies the mode for filling the airspace area.␀␀␀No labels will be displayed.␀␀␀␀All labels will be displayed.␀␀␀Default␀This selects the best performing option for your hardware. In fact it favours 'fill padding' except for PPC 2000 system.␀␀␀␀Fill all␀␀␀␀Transparently fills the airspace colour over the whole area.␀␀␀␀Fill padding␀␀␀␀Draws a solid outline with a half transparent border around the airspace.␀␀␀No fill␀Don't fill the airspace area.␀␀␀All on␀␀All airspaces are displayed.␀␀␀␀Clip␀␀␀␀Display airspaces below the clip altitude.␀␀Display airspaces within a margin of the glider.␀␀␀␀All below␀␀␀Display airspaces below the glider or within a margin.␀␀AcknowledgementTime␀RepetitiveSound␀WarnTime␀␀␀␀ClipAlt␀AltitudeMode␀␀␀␀AltMargin␀␀␀AirspaceLabelSelection␀␀AirspaceFillMode␀␀␀␀AirspaceBlackOutline␀␀␀␀AirspaceWarnDialog␀␀AirspaceWarn␀␀␀␀Enable and select the position of the thermal assistant when overlayed on the main screen.␀␀Disable thermal assistant.␀␀Bottom left␀Show thermal assistant in bottom left.␀␀Bottom left (avoid InfoBoxes)␀␀␀Show thermal assistant in bottom left, above or to the right of InfoBoxes (if present).␀Bottom right␀␀␀␀Show thermal assistant in bottom right.␀Bottom right (avoid InfoBoxes)␀␀Show thermal assistant in bottom right, above or to the left of InfoBoxes (if present).␀Top left␀␀␀␀Show thermal assistant in top left.␀Top right␀␀␀Show thermal assistant in top right.␀␀␀␀Center top␀␀Show thermal assistant in center top.␀␀␀Top left (avoid InfoBoxes)␀␀Show thermal assistant in top left (avoid InfoBoxes).␀␀␀Top right (avoid InfoBoxes)␀Show thermal assistant in top right (avoid InfoBoxes).␀␀Center top (avoid InfoBoxes)␀␀␀␀Show thermal assistant in center top (avoid InfoBoxes).␀TAPosition␀␀This enables the display of the thermal profile (climb band) display on the map.␀␀␀␀Final glide bar␀If set to "On" the final glide will always be shown, if set to "Auto" it will be shown when approaching the final glide possibility.␀␀␀␀Final glide bar MC0␀If set to "On" the final glide bar will show a second arrow indicating the required height to reach the final waypoint at MC zero.␀␀Vario bar␀␀␀If set to "On" the vario bar will be shown.␀Disable final glide bar.␀␀␀␀Always show final glide bar.␀␀␀␀Show final glide bar if approaching final glide range.␀␀EnableVarioBar␀␀FinalGlideBarDisplayMode␀␀␀␀EnableFinalGlideBarMC0␀␀EnableThermalProfile␀␀␀␀Enable the traffic radar and choose its position on the main screen. The track bearing of the target relative to the track bearing of the aircraft is displayed as an arrow head, and a triangle pointing up or down shows the relative altitude of the target relative to you. In all modes, the color of the target indicates the threat level.␀␀␀Auto close FLARM␀␀␀␀Setting this to "On" will automatically close the FLARM dialog if there is no traffic. "Off" will keep the dialog open even without current traffic.␀␀␀␀No position target␀␀This parameter enables or disables the No Position Target Distance Ring in Flarm Radar␀␀Spoken alerts␀␀␀Speak the collision alarms of FLARM, with the urgency, the clock position and whether the traffic is above or below: "Traffic urgent! Three o'clock. Above." Obstacle and alert zone alarms are not spoken.␀Test spoken alert␀␀␀Alert screen␀␀␀␀Cover the screen with the direction, height and distance of the traffic when a collision alarm reaches this level, with buttons to dismiss it and to mute that aircraft for a minute. While circling, only an urgent alarm shows it.␀␀␀␀Disable the traffic radar.␀␀Auto (follow InfoBoxes)␀Center bottom␀␀␀Center bottom (avoid InfoBoxes)␀Important and urgent␀␀␀␀Urgent only␀FlarmLocation␀␀␀FlarmAlertPopup␀FlarmSpokenAlerts␀␀␀AutoCloseFlarmDialog␀␀␀␀EnableFLARMGauge␀␀␀␀NoPositionTargetDistanceRing␀␀␀␀Display type␀␀␀␀Select the display technology. E-ink modes disable kinetic and smooth scrolling for slow refresh screens.␀␀␀Display resolution␀␀The display resolution is used to adapt line widths, font size, landable size and more.␀%u dpi␀␀Display orientation␀Rotate the display on devices that support it.␀␀Reverse Portrait␀␀␀␀Reverse Landscape␀␀␀LCD␀Conventional LCD or OLED. Full scrolling animations.␀␀␀␀E-ink␀␀␀Monochrome electronic paper. Disables kinetic and smooth scrolling.␀Color e-ink␀Color electronic paper. Disables kinetic and smooth scrolling like monochrome e-ink.␀␀␀␀DisplayOrientation␀␀DisplayType␀CustomDPI␀␀␀Speed arrows␀␀␀␀Whether to show speed command arrows on the vario gauge. In cruise mode, arrows pointing up command slow down; arrows pointing down command speed up.␀␀␀Show average␀␀␀␀Whether to show the average climb rate. In cruise mode, this switches to showing the average netto airmass rate.␀␀␀␀Show MacReady␀␀␀Whether to show the MacCready setting.␀␀Show gross␀␀Whether to show the gross climb rate.␀␀␀If true, the vario gauge will display a hollow averager needle. During cruise, this needle displays the average netto value. During circling, this needle displays the average gross value.␀Show bugs␀␀␀Whether to show the bugs percentage.␀␀␀␀Show ballast␀␀␀␀Whether to show the ballast percentage.␀Thermal Averager needle␀If true, the vario gauge will display a thermal averager needle instead of the current climb-rate needle. During cruise, this needle displays the last thermal average netto value. During circling, this needle displays the average net value.␀␀␀␀AppAveThermalNeedle␀AppAveNeedle␀␀␀␀AppGaugeVarioGross␀␀AppGaugeVarioBallast␀␀␀␀AppGaugeVarioBugs␀␀␀AppGaugeVarioMc␀AppGaugeVarioAvgText␀␀␀␀AppGaugeVarioSpeedToFly␀Auto MC mode␀␀␀␀This option defines which auto MacCready algorithm is used.␀Wave assistant␀␀Enable detection and display of wave lift. When enabled, wave sources are identified and shown on the map.␀␀Block speed to fly␀␀If enabled, the command speed in cruise is set to the MacCready speed to fly in no vertical air-mass movement. If disabled, the command speed in cruise is set to the dolphin speed to fly, equivalent to the MacCready speed with vertical air-mass movement.␀␀Predict wind drift␀␀Account for wind drift for the predicted circling duration. This reduces the arrival height for legs with head wind.␀␀␀␀Nav. by baro altitude␀␀␀When enabled and if connected to a barometric altimeter, barometric altitude is used for all navigation functions. Otherwise GPS altitude is used.␀␀GR average period␀␀␀Here you can decide on how many seconds of flight this calculation must be done. Normally for gliders a good value is 90-120 seconds, and for paragliders 15 seconds.␀␀␀Speed command switch␀␀␀␀How XCSoar uses the variometer's cruise/climb report.␀␀␀Cruise/Circling period␀␀How many seconds of turning before changing from cruise to circling mode.␀␀␀Circling/Cruise period␀␀How many seconds of flying straight before changing from circling to cruise mode.␀␀␀XCSoar decides circling from the turn. The variometer report is not used.␀␀␀Flight mode␀While flying, the variometer's cruise/climb report is the flight mode. Cruise ends the climb and restarts the average. Climb starts a new thermal. Final glide can still appear in cruise. On a Vega this is the circling output.␀␀␀Display mode␀␀␀␀The page changes and the flight mode does not. Cruise shows the cruise page, including while turning, and hides final glide. Climb shows the vario page. On a Vega this is the speed-command switch.␀␀␀␀15 s␀␀␀␀Preferred period for paragliders.␀␀␀60 s␀␀␀␀90 s␀␀␀␀Preferred period for gliders.␀␀␀2 min␀␀␀3 min␀␀␀Final glide␀Adjusts MC for the fastest arrival. For contest sprint tasks, the MacCready is adjusted in order to cover the greatest distance in the remaining time and reach the finish height.␀␀Trending average climb␀␀Sets MC to the trending average climb rate based on all climbs.␀Both␀␀␀␀Uses trending average during task, then fastest arrival when in final glide mode.␀␀␀WaveAssistant␀␀␀PredictWindDrift␀␀␀␀CirclingToCruiseModeSwitchThreshold␀CruiseToCirclingModeSwitchThreshold␀SpeedCommandSwitch␀␀AutoMcMode␀␀EnableNavBaroAltitude␀␀␀BlockSpeedToFly␀AverEffTime␀Use final glide mode␀␀␀␀Controls whether the "final glide" InfoBox mode should be used on "auto" pages.␀Events␀␀The Input Events file defines the menu system and how XCSoar responds to button presses and events from external devices.␀␀␀Language␀␀␀␀The language options selects translations for English texts to other languages. Select English for a native interface or Automatic to localise XCSoar according to the system settings.␀English␀*.mo␀␀␀␀Menu timeout␀␀␀␀This determines how long menus will appear on screen if the user does not make any button presses or interacts with the computer.␀␀␀Text input style␀␀␀␀Determines how the user is prompted for text input (filename, teamcode etc.)␀␀␀␀Show Quick Guide␀␀␀␀If enabled, the Quick Guide is shown when XCSoar starts.␀␀␀␀Show release notes␀␀If enabled, the What's New page is shown on the next startup.␀␀␀Warn when radar expires␀If enabled, a warning is shown when the rain radar overlay could not be refreshed and was removed from the map.␀Safety disclaimer accepted␀␀Whether the safety disclaimer has been accepted for this version.␀␀␀Keyboard␀␀␀␀HighScore Style␀HideRadarStaleWarning␀␀␀MenuTimeout␀AppTextInputStyle␀␀␀Show QuickMenu button␀␀␀Dark mode␀␀␀Text size␀␀␀Viewing distance␀␀␀␀Auto shrinks type when the short edge is under 3.2 inches. Handheld always uses that closer size. Panel keeps full-size type.␀␀␀Tab dialog style␀␀␀␀InfoBox geometry␀␀␀␀A list of possible InfoBox layouts. Do some trials to find the best for your screen size.␀␀␀InfoBox title size␀␀Zoom factor for InfoBox title and comment text␀␀Colored InfoBoxes␀␀␀If true, certain InfoBoxes will have coloured text. For example, the active waypoint InfoBox will be blue when the glider is above final glide.␀InfoBox theme␀␀␀InfoBox border␀␀Message display␀Show Menu button␀␀␀␀Show the Menu button␀␀␀␀Show Zoom button␀␀␀␀Show the Zoom button␀␀␀␀Show the QuickMenu button␀␀␀Use the system-wide setting␀Black text on white background␀␀White text on black background␀␀Follow global␀␀␀Use the same light/dark mode as the overall UI.␀Light␀␀␀Always use dark text on a light InfoBox background.␀Dark␀␀␀␀Always use light text on a dark InfoBox background.␀Draws boxes around each InfoBox.␀␀␀␀Tab␀Draws a tab at the top of the InfoBox across the title.␀Shaded␀␀Glass␀␀␀Center␀␀Center the status message boxes.␀␀␀␀Show status message boxes in the top left corner.␀␀␀Show text on tabbed dialogs.␀␀␀␀Icons␀␀␀Show icons on tabbed dialogs.␀␀␀Shrink type when the short edge is under 3.2 inches.␀␀␀␀Handheld␀␀␀␀Always use the closer-view size.␀␀␀␀Panel␀␀␀Keep full-size type, for a screen read at arm's length.␀8 Split␀10 Split␀␀␀␀12 Split in 3 rows␀␀15 Split in 3 rows␀␀18 Split in 3 rows␀␀8 Bottom or Right␀␀␀8 Bottom + Vario (Portrait)␀8 Top or Left␀␀␀8 Top + Vario (Portrait)␀␀␀␀9 Right + Vario (Landscape)␀9 Left + Right + Vario (Landscape)␀␀12 Left + 3 Right Vario (Landscape)␀5 Right (Square)␀␀␀␀10 Bottom or Right␀␀12 Bottom or Right␀␀10 Top or Left␀␀12 Top or Left␀␀16 Right (Landscape)␀␀␀␀24 Bottom or Right␀␀4 Top or Left␀␀␀4 Bottom or Right␀␀␀ShowQuickMenuButton␀ShowZoomButton␀␀ShowMenuButton␀␀AppDialogTabStyle␀␀␀AppStatusMessageAlignment␀␀␀DarkMode␀␀␀␀ViewingDistance␀UIScale␀Name of the pilot in command, recorded in the IGC flight log.␀␀␀CoPilot name␀␀␀␀The co-pilot name recorded in the IGC flight log.␀␀␀Crew weight default␀Default for all weight loaded to the glider beyond the empty weight and besides the water ballast.␀␀Time step cruise␀␀␀␀This is the time interval between logged points when not circling.␀␀Time step circling␀␀This is the time interval between logged points when circling.␀␀Auto. logger␀␀␀␀Enables the automatic starting and stopping of logger on takeoff and landing respectively. Disable when flying paragliders.␀NMEA Logger␀Enable the NMEA logger on startup? If this option is disabled, the NMEA logger can still be started manually.␀␀␀Log book␀␀␀␀Logs each start and landing.␀␀␀␀Logger ID␀␀␀The three-letter logger ID used in the IGC filename.␀␀␀␀Start only␀␀EnableNMEALogger␀␀␀␀EnableFlightLogger␀␀AutoLogger␀␀LoggerTimeStepCircling␀␀LoggerTimeStepCruise␀␀␀␀LoggerID␀␀␀␀Determines how the screen is rotated with the glider␀␀␀␀Determines how the screen is rotated with the glider while circling␀Circling zoom␀␀␀If enabled, then the map will zoom in automatically when entering circling mode and zoom out automatically when leaving circling mode.␀␀Map shift reference␀Determines what is used to shift the glider from the map center␀Glider position offset␀␀Defines the location of the glider drawn on the screen in percent from the screen edge.␀Max. auto zoom distance␀The upper limit for auto zoom distance.␀Distinct page zoom␀␀Maintain one map zoom level on each page.␀␀␀Disable adjustments.␀␀␀␀Track␀␀␀Use a recent average of the ground track as basis.␀␀Use the current target waypoint as basis.␀␀␀The moving map display will be rotated so the glider's track is oriented up.␀␀␀␀The moving map display will be rotated so the glider's heading is oriented up.␀␀The moving map display will always be orientated north to south and the glider icon will be rotated to show its course.␀The moving map display will be rotated so the navigation target is oriented up.␀The moving map display will be rotated so the wind is always oriented up to down. (can be useful for wave flying)␀␀␀GliderScreenPosition␀␀␀␀MapShiftBias␀␀␀␀OrientationCircling␀OrientationCruise␀␀␀MaxAutoZoomDistance␀CircleZoom␀␀Connecting␀␀Status unknown␀␀WiFi service is not available.␀␀ESPHome␀Connected to %s␀This page shows WiFi status. Use WiFi list to scan and connect.␀Checking WiFi...␀␀␀␀Connectivity␀␀␀␀Current network connectivity state.␀IP address␀␀IPv4 address of the active WiFi interface.␀␀Backend␀WiFi service used by the device.␀␀␀␀WiFi Enabled␀␀␀␀Turns WiFi on or off until the next restart.␀␀␀␀Automatic network␀␀␀Join a SkyEcho when one is in range, otherwise another saved network. Joining a network in the WiFi list turns this off until the next restart.␀WiFi List␀␀␀RASP weather layer to display on this map page.␀No RASP file loaded␀SkySight layer␀␀SkySight layer used when this page overlay is SkySight.␀No SkySight layers selected␀Layer / Level␀␀␀Select a RASP or EDL map overlay to configure its layer or level for this page.␀A custom InfoBox set␀␀␀␀Main area␀␀␀Specifies what should be displayed in the main area.␀␀␀␀Specifies which InfoBoxes should be displayed on this page.␀Bottom area␀Specifies what should be displayed below the main area. Weather controls require a weather map overlay.␀Map overlay␀Optional weather overlay on map pages. Use with Weather controls in the bottom area for in-flight adjustment.␀␀␀Select a weather map overlay to configure its layer or level for this page.␀For cruise mode. Displayed when 'Auto' is selected and glider is below final glide altitude.␀␀␀␀For circling mode. Displayed when 'Auto' is selected and glider is circling.␀␀␀␀For final glide mode. Displayed when 'Auto' is selected and glider is above final glide altitude.␀␀␀Nothing␀Cross section␀␀␀Weather controls␀␀␀␀Displays either the Circling, Cruise, or Final glide InfoBoxes.␀Show fullscreen (no InfoBoxes)␀␀Map (north-up)␀␀Route mode␀␀Route climb␀When enabled and MC is positive, route planning allows climbs between the aircraft location and destination.␀␀␀␀Route ceiling␀␀␀When enabled, route planning climbs are limited to ceiling defined by greater of current aircraft altitude plus 500 m and the thermal ceiling. If disabled, climbs are unlimited.␀␀␀Reach mode␀␀How calculations are performed of the reach of the glider with respect to terrain.␀␀Reach polar␀Which MacCready is used for terrain reach, landable colours, abort and the Alternates list. This still applies when Reach mode is Off. The working line always uses Task MC. Alternate InfoBoxes and waypoint Alt. diff. Safety MC always use Safety MC.␀␀␀␀Reach display␀␀␀No reach overlay on the map.␀␀␀␀Terrain line␀␀␀␀How far you can glide to the ground. Uses the Reach polar.␀␀Terrain shade␀␀␀Shades the map beyond glide to the ground. Uses the Reach polar.␀␀␀␀Working line␀␀␀␀How far you can glide and still arrive at typical thermal-base height from this flight. Always uses Task MC.␀␀␀␀Working line, terrain line␀␀Working line to typical thermal-base height using Task MC, plus terrain line to the ground using the Reach polar.␀␀␀Working line, terrain shade␀Working line to typical thermal-base height using Task MC, plus shade beyond the ground using the Reach polar.␀␀Uses task MacCready value.␀␀Safety MC␀␀␀Uses safety MacCready value.␀␀␀␀Disables map reach (terrain line and shade). Abort, alternates and landable arrival still use the Reach polar.␀␀Straight␀␀␀␀The reach is from straight line paths from the glider. Landable colours and the Alternates list use this reach.␀Turning␀The reach is calculated allowing turns around terrain obstacles. Landable colours and the Alternates list use this reach.␀␀␀Neither airspace nor terrain is used for route planning.␀␀␀␀Routes will avoid terrain.␀␀Routes will avoid airspace.␀Routes will avoid airspace and terrain.␀ReachPolarMode␀␀TurningReach␀␀␀␀RoutePlannerUseCeiling␀␀RoutePlannerAllowClimb␀␀RoutePlannerMode␀␀␀␀FinalGlideTerrain␀␀␀%s:1␀␀␀␀The MacCready used for terrain reach, landable colours, abort and the Alternates list when Reach polar is Safety MC. Alternate InfoBoxes and waypoint Alt. diff. Safety MC always use this value. Speed-to-fly is not affected. Higher values treat fewer fields as reachable.␀␀No glide polar is configured, so no glide ratio can be shown. Select a plane first.␀The glide ratio next to each value is the best glide ratio in still air at that MacCready setting, including bugs, ballast and polar degradation.␀␀␀Active polar: %s␀␀␀␀Turn back marker␀␀␀␀Arrival height␀␀The height above terrain that the glider should arrive at for a safe landing.␀␀␀Terrain height␀␀The height above terrain that the glider must clear during final glide.␀Alternates mode␀Determines sorting of alternates in the alternates dialog and in abort mode.␀␀␀␀Polar degradation␀␀␀A permanent polar degradation. 0% means no degradation, 50% indicates the glider's sink rate is doubled.␀␀␀␀Auto bugs␀␀␀If enabled, adds 1% to the bugs setting after each full hour while flying.␀␀Startup Task MC␀The MacCready used at startup for speed-to-fly and task calculations. Changing it here also sets the live MacCready. Terrain reach, landable colours, abort and the Alternates list follow Reach polar, not this value unless Reach polar is Task MC.␀␀␀Safety MC GR␀␀␀␀STF risk factor␀The STF risk factor reduces the MacCready setting used to calculate speed to fly as the glider gets low, in order to compensate for risk. Set to 0.0 for no compensation, 1.0 scales MC linearly with current height (with reference to height of the maximum climb). If considered, 0.3 is recommended.␀␀␀␀Show a green triangle on the map along the current track indicating the furthest point from which the active task waypoint or Goto target can still be reached with the current altitude and conditions. The triangle is only shown during cruise when the target is reachable.␀Simple␀␀Reachable airfields are listed first (nearest at top), then outlanding sites (nearest at top).␀␀Reachable airfields are listed first (smallest detour to the active turnpoint at top), then outlanding sites.␀␀␀Home␀␀␀␀Reachable airfields are listed first (smallest detour toward home at top), then outlanding sites.␀␀␀RiskGamma␀␀␀AbortTaskMode␀␀␀SafetyMacCready␀TaskMacCready␀␀␀TurnBackMarkerEnabled␀␀␀SafetyAltitudeTerrain␀␀␀SafetyAltitudeArrival␀␀␀AutoBugs␀␀␀␀PolarDegradation␀␀␀␀XCSoar data path␀␀␀␀Click to view full path␀Map database␀␀␀␀The name of the file (.xcm) containing terrain, topography, and optionally waypoints, their details and airspaces.␀␀Primary waypoints files.  Supported file types are Cambridge/WinPilot files (.dat), Zander files (.wpz) or SeeYou files (.cup, .cupx).␀␀Watched WPTs␀␀␀␀Waypoint files containing special waypoints for which additional computations like calculation of arrival height in map display always takes place. Useful for waypoints like known reliable thermal sources (e.g. powerplants) or mountain passes.␀WPT A/F details␀The files may contain extracts from enroute supplements or other contributed information about individual waypoints and airfields.␀␀List of active airspace files. Use the Add and Remove buttons to activate or deactivate airspace files respectively. Supported file types are: Openair (.openair /.txt /.air), and Tim Newport-Pearce (.sua).␀␀␀FLARM database␀␀The name of the file containing information about registered FLARM devices.␀Regional Atmospheric Soaring Prediction file providing weather forecasts for soaring. Displays color-coded map overlays for thermal strength, boundary layer winds, cloud cover, and other soaring-relevant parameters at various forecast times throughout the day.␀␀␀␀The checklist file containing pre-flight and other checklists.␀␀User repositories␀␀␀List of additional user repository URIs, separated by '|' character.␀␀␀␀UserRepositoriesList␀␀␀␀Ground track␀␀␀␀Display the ground track as a grey line on the map.␀FLARM Traffic␀␀␀This enables the display of FLARM traffic on the map window.␀␀␀␀Fade traffic␀␀␀␀Keep showing traffic for a while after it has disappeared.␀␀Trail length␀␀␀␀Determines whether and how long a snail trail is drawn behind the glider.␀␀␀Trail drift␀Determines whether the snail trail is drifted with the wind when displayed in circling mode at near map scales. Switched Off, the snail trail stays uncompensated for wind drift. In circling this also applies to the projected track curve: On keeps it relative to the air mass; Off includes wind.␀␀Trail type␀␀Sets the type of the snail trail display.␀␀␀Trail scaled␀␀␀␀If set to ON the snail trail width is scaled according to the vario signal.␀Detour cost markers␀If the aircraft heading deviates from the current waypoint, markers are displayed at points ahead of the aircraft. The value of each marker is the extra distance required to reach that point as a percentage of straight-line distance to the waypoint.␀␀␀Aircraft symbol␀Wind arrow␀␀Determines the way the wind arrow is drawn on the map.␀␀Online traffic on map␀␀␀Show traffic from SkyLines and XCSoar Cloud on the map.␀Distance rings␀␀Display distance rings around the aircraft on the map.␀␀No online traffic is drawn.␀Draws the traffic symbol only.␀␀Symbol and Name␀Draws the traffic symbol with name.␀No wind arrow is drawn.␀Arrow head␀␀Draws an arrow head only.␀␀␀Full arrow␀␀Draws an arrow head with a dashed arrow line.␀␀␀Simplified line graphics, black with white contours.␀␀␀␀Simple (large)␀␀Enlarged simple graphics.␀␀␀Detailed␀␀␀␀Detailed rendered aircraft graphics.␀␀␀␀Simplified hang glider as line graphics, white with black contours.␀Simplified para glider as line graphics, white with black contours.␀Vario #1␀␀␀␀Within lift areas lines get displayed green and thicker, while sinking lines are shown brown and thin. Zero lift is presented as a grey line.␀␀␀Vario #1 (with dots)␀␀␀␀The same colour scheme as the previous, but with dotted lines while sinking.␀␀␀␀Vario #2␀␀␀␀The climb colour for this scheme is orange to red, sinking is displayed as light blue to dark blue. Zero lift is presented as a yellow line.␀␀␀␀Vario #2 (with dots)␀␀␀␀Vario-scaled dots and lines␀Vario-scaled dots with lines. Orange to red = climb. Light blue to dark blue = sink. Zero lift is presented as a yellow line.␀␀␀Vario E-ink␀E-ink friendly color scheme, lighter and thicker dots means lift while darker and thinner means sink.␀␀␀The colour scheme corresponds to the height.␀␀␀␀Disable display of ground track line.␀␀␀Always display ground track line.␀␀␀Display ground track line if there is a significant difference to plane heading.␀␀␀␀OnlineTrafficMapMode␀␀␀␀AircraftSymbol␀␀WindArrowStyle␀␀SnailType␀␀␀SnailWidthScale␀FadeTraffic␀EnableFLARMDisplay␀␀DisplayTrackBearing␀DetourCostMarker␀␀␀␀TrailDrift␀␀SnailTrail␀␀Start max. speed margin␀Maximum speed above maximum start speed to tolerate. Set to 0 for no tolerance.␀Start max. height margin␀␀␀␀Maximum height above maximum start height to tolerate. Set to 0 for no tolerance.␀␀␀Number of minutes start remains open after Pilot Event and PEV wait time.0 means start will never close after it opens.␀PEVStartWindow␀␀PEVStartWaitTime␀␀␀␀NavigateNearest␀StartHeightRef␀␀StartMaxSpeedMargin␀StartMaxHeightMargin␀␀␀␀StartMaxSpeed␀␀␀StartMaxHeight␀␀FinishHeightRef␀FinishMinHeight␀Default start type for new tasks you create.␀␀␀␀Default radius or gate width of the start zone for new tasks.␀␀␀Default finish type for new tasks you create.␀␀␀Default radius or gate width of the finish zone in new tasks.␀␀␀Turn point␀␀Default turn point type for new tasks you create.␀␀␀Default radius of turnpoint cylinders and sectors in new tasks.␀Default task type for new tasks you create.␀Default AAT min. time for new AAT tasks.␀␀␀␀Optimisation margin␀Safety margin for AAT task optimisation. Optimisation seeks to complete the task at the minimum time plus this margin time.␀AATTimeMargin␀␀␀AATMinTime␀␀TaskType␀␀␀␀FinishRadius␀␀␀␀FinishType␀␀TurnpointRadius␀TurnpointType␀␀␀StartRadius␀StartType␀␀␀Disable contest calculations␀␀␀␀Conforms to FAI triangle rules. Three turns and common start and finish. No leg less than 28% of total except for tasks longer than 500km: No leg less than 25% or larger than 45%.␀Up to seven points including start and finish, finish height must not be lower than start height less 1000 meters.␀␀The most recent contest with Sprint task rules.␀A combination of Classic and FAI rules. 30% of the FAI score are added to the Classic score.␀␀␀␀Deutsche Meisterschaft im Streckensegelflug.␀␀␀␀PG online contest with different track values: Free flight - 1 km = 1.0 point; flat triangle - 1 km = 1.2 p; FAI triangle - 1 km = 1.4 p.␀␀␀European PG online contest of the DHV organization. Pretty much the same as the XContest rules, but with different track values: 1 km = 1.5 points, 1.75 p and 2.0 p for FAI triangles respectively.␀␀␀␀Austrian online glider contest. Tracks around max. six waypoints are scored. The bounding box part with 1 km = 1.0 point and the additional zick-zack part with 1 km = 0.5 p.␀␀␀FFVP Federal Cup (NetCoupe) on WeGlide. The scored path has at most three turnpoints between start and finish and at least 25 km total. Points are proportional to credited distance, 100 divided by the glider handicap (DAeC-style index), and a success factor of 1.0 for a free flight or 1.2 for a task declared electronically before takeoff. XCSoar live scoring uses a success factor of 1.0 only, not the 1.2 multiplier for declared tasks.␀␀WeGlide combines multiple scoring systems in the WeGlide Free contest. The free score is a combination of the free distance score and the area bonus. For the area bonus, the scoring program determines the largest FAI triangle and the largest Out & Return distance that can be fitted into the flight route.␀␀␀A start point, one turn point and a finish point are chosen from the flight path such that the distance between the start point and the turn point is maximized.␀␀␀␀LVZC Charron.online, 5 legs under 200km 6 legs above. Minimum leg distance is 20km, 5 points per km.␀␀␀␀Select the rules used for calculating optimal points for a contest.␀Predict Contest␀If enabled, then the next task point is included in the score calculation, assuming that you will reach it.␀FAI triangle areas␀␀Show FAI triangle areas on the map.␀FAI triangle threshold␀␀Specifies which threshold is used for "large" FAI triangles.␀␀␀␀95% dist. rule helpers␀␀Show helpers for Argentinean Federation "95% distance" rule. The AAT Distance Around Target InfoBox will show projected distance vs. maximum and change colors as you approach 95%.␀750km (FAI)␀500km (OLC, DMSt)␀␀␀Show95PercentRuleHelpers␀␀␀␀FAITriangleThreshold␀␀␀␀ShowFAITriangleAreas␀␀␀␀PredictContest␀␀ContestEnumLayout␀␀␀OLCRules␀␀␀␀Terrain shown␀␀␀Terrain hidden␀␀Topography shown␀␀␀␀Topography hidden␀␀␀Draw a digital elevation terrain on the map.␀␀␀␀Topography display␀␀Draw topographical features (roads, rivers, lakes etc.) on the map.␀Terrain colors␀␀Defines the color ramp used in terrain rendering.␀␀␀Slope shading␀␀␀The terrain can be shaded among slopes to indicate either wind direction, sun position, a geographically fixed shading from North-West, or a screen-relative fixed shading from top left.␀␀␀Terrain contrast␀␀␀␀Defines the amount of Phong shading in the terrain rendering. Use large values to emphasise terrain slope, smaller values if flying in steep mountains.␀Terrain brightness␀␀Defines the brightness (whiteness) of the terrain rendering. This controls the average illumination of the terrain.␀Draw contour lines on the terrain. Contour mode controls density of contour lines.␀␀No contour lines␀␀␀␀Mountains␀␀␀For steep mountain terrain, 256m minimum spacing␀␀␀␀Highlands␀␀␀Medium density, with 64m minimum spacing␀␀␀␀Lowlands␀␀␀␀More line density for gentler slopes. 16m minimum spacing␀␀␀Superfine␀␀␀Maximum density contour lines down to 8m spacing␀␀␀␀Fixed 256m␀␀Fixed 256m spacing, no zoom dependence␀␀Fixed 128m␀␀Fixed 128m spacing, no zoom dependence␀␀Fixed 64m␀␀␀Fixed 64m spacing, no zoom dependence␀␀␀Fixed (North-West)␀␀Sun␀Fixed (Top Left)␀␀␀␀Low lands␀␀␀Mountainous␀Imhof 7␀Imhof 4␀Imhof 12␀␀␀␀Imhof Atlas␀ICAO␀␀␀␀Vibrant␀Grey␀␀␀␀White␀␀␀Sandstone␀␀␀Pastel␀␀Italian Avioportolano VFR Chart␀German DFS VFR Chart␀␀␀␀French SIA VFR Chart␀␀␀␀High Contrast␀␀␀High Contrast low lands␀Very low lands␀␀TerrainRamp␀TerrainBrightness␀␀␀TerrainContrast␀DrawTopology␀␀␀␀TerrainContours␀SlopeShadingType␀␀␀␀DrawTerrain␀Preset␀␀Load a set of units.␀␀␀␀My individual set of units.␀Aircraft/Wind speed␀Units used for airspeed and ground speed. A separate unit is available for task speeds.␀Units used for horizontal distances e.g. range to waypoint, distance to go.␀Units used for vertical speeds (variometer).␀␀␀␀Units used for altitude and heights.␀␀␀␀Units used for temperature.␀Units used for task speeds.␀Units used for pressures.␀␀␀Units used for mass.␀␀␀␀Units used for wing loading.␀␀␀␀Lat./Lon.␀␀␀Units used for latitude and longitude.␀␀Rotation␀␀␀␀Unit used for rotation.␀DDMMSS␀␀DDMMSS.s␀␀␀␀DDMM.mmm␀␀␀␀DD.ddddd␀␀␀␀UTM␀knots␀␀␀feet␀␀␀␀ft/min␀␀TaskSpeedUnit␀␀␀LatLonUnits␀RotationUnit␀␀␀␀MassUnit␀␀␀␀WingLoadingUnit␀LiftUnit␀␀␀␀DistanceUnit␀␀␀␀TemperatureUnit␀AltitudeUnit␀␀␀␀%s (UTC%c%s)␀␀␀␀Local time source␀␀␀Selects where XCSoar gets the offset between UTC and local time from.␀␀␀Time zone␀␀␀The time zone of the airfield you are flying at.␀␀␀␀Manual UTC offset␀␀␀The UTC offset field allows the UTC local time offset to be specified. It keeps the value you entered even while another local time source is selected. The local time is displayed below, along with the UTC offset which is currently in effect.␀␀Use GPS time␀␀␀␀If enabled sets the clock of the computer to the GPS time once a fix is set. This is only necessary if your computer does not have a real-time clock with battery backup or your computer frequently runs out of battery power or otherwise loses time.␀Use the fixed UTC offset entered below.  It has to be corrected manually whenever daylight saving time begins or ends.␀␀Use the time zone which is configured in the operating system, and keep following it across daylight saving time changes and when travelling to another time zone.␀␀Use the time zone selected below.  XCSoar knows its daylight saving time rules and applies the transitions on its own, which means the setting does not need to be corrected twice a year.␀␀SetSystemTimeFromGPS␀␀␀␀TimeZone␀␀␀␀LocalTimeSource␀UTCOffsetSigned␀Label format␀␀␀␀Determines how labels are displayed with each waypoint␀␀Determines how arrival height is displayed in waypoint labels␀␀␀Label style␀Waypoint icon size␀␀Size of waypoint symbols on the map as a percentage of the built-in artwork (list dialogs keep a fixed row icon size).␀␀Landable symbols␀␀␀␀Three styles are available: Purple circles (WinPilot style), a high contrast (monochrome) style, or orange. The rendering differs for landable field and airport. All styles mark the waypoints within reach green.␀Detailed landables␀␀[Off] Display fixed icons for landables.
[On] Show landables with variable information like runway length and heading.␀␀Landable size␀␀␀A percentage to select the size landables are displayed on the map.␀Scale runway length␀[Off] Display fixed length for runways.
[On] Scale displayed runway length based on real length.␀␀␀␀Purple circle␀␀␀Airports and outlanding fields are displayed as purple circles. If the waypoint is reachable a bigger green circle is added behind the purple one. If the waypoint is blocked by a mountain the green circle will be red instead.␀␀␀B/W␀Airports and outlanding fields are displayed in white/grey. If the waypoint is reachable the color is changed to green. If the waypoint is blocked by a mountain the color is changed to red instead.␀␀␀Traffic lights␀␀Airports and outlanding fields are displayed in the colors of a traffic light. Green if reachable, Orange if blocked by mountain and red if not reachable at all.␀␀␀Rounded rectangle␀␀␀Outlined␀␀␀␀No arrival height is displayed.␀Straight glide␀␀Straight glide arrival height (no terrain is considered).␀␀␀Terrain avoidance glide␀Arrival height considering terrain avoidance. Requires "Reach mode: Turning" in "Glide Computer > Route" settings.␀␀Straight & terrain glide␀␀␀␀Both arrival heights are displayed. Requires "Reach mode: Turning" in "Glide Computer > Route" settings.␀␀␀␀Required glide ratio␀␀␀␀Required GR & terrain glide␀Both Required glide ratio and terrain avoidance height are displayed. Requires "Reach mode: Turning" in "Glide Computer > Route" settings.␀␀Task waypoints & airfields␀␀All waypoints part of a task and all airfields will be displayed.␀␀␀Task waypoints & landables␀␀All waypoints part of a task and all landables will be displayed.␀␀␀Task waypoints␀␀All waypoints part of a task will be displayed.␀Full name␀␀␀The full name of each waypoint is displayed.␀␀␀␀First word of name␀␀The first word of the waypoint name is displayed.␀␀␀First 3 letters␀The first 3 letters of the waypoint name are displayed.␀First 5 letters␀The first 5 letters of the waypoint name are displayed.␀No waypoint name is displayed.␀␀The short name of each waypoint is displayed. If unavailable, the first five letters of the full name are displayed.␀␀␀␀AppScaleRunwayLength␀␀␀␀MapWaypointIconScale␀␀␀␀AppLandableRenderingScale␀␀␀AppUseSWLandablesRendering␀␀AppIndLandable␀␀WayPointLabelStyle␀␀WayPointLabelSelection␀␀WaypointArrivalHeightDisplay␀␀␀␀DisplayText␀Allow download of declared tasks from Weglide in the Task Manager.␀␀Automatic Upload␀␀␀␀Asks whether to upload flight to Weglide, after flight is downloaded from external logger.␀␀Take this from your WeGlide Profile. Or set to 0 if not used.␀␀␀Pilot date of birth␀WeGlideAutomaticUpload␀␀Radius of the OZ cylinder.␀␀Width of the start/finish gate.␀Radius of the OZ sector.␀␀␀␀Angle of the OZ sector␀␀Start radial␀␀␀␀Start radial of the OZ area␀Finish radial␀␀␀Finish radial of the OZ area␀␀␀␀Inner radius␀␀␀␀Inner radius of the OZ sector.␀␀(Add Alternate Start)␀␀␀Alternate Start Points␀␀For AAT tasks, this setting can be used to adjust the target points within the AAT sectors. Larger values move the target points to produce larger task distances, smaller values move the target points to produce smaller task distances.␀Radial␀␀For AAT tasks, this setting can be used to adjust the target points within the AAT sectors. Positive values rotate the range line clockwise, negative values rotate the range line counterclockwise.␀␀␀␀%.0fÂ°␀␀Estimated time en-route to the next AAT target.␀Delta T␀AAT Delta Time - Difference between estimated task time and AAT minimum time. Colored red if negative (expected arrival too early), or blue if in sector and can turn now with estimated arrival time greater than AAT time plus 5 minutes.␀V rem.␀␀V ach␀␀␀AA Speed - Assigned Area Task average speed achievable around target points remaining in minimum AAT time.␀␀Optimized␀␀␀Alternate Mode: Auto␀␀␀␀Select as Alternate␀Set Active Frequency␀␀␀␀Set Standby Frequency␀␀␀No alternates available␀Alternates 1␀␀␀␀Alternates 2␀␀␀␀EDL weather␀%s is not available in this build because the OpenGL renderer is not available.␀EDL␀%04u-%02u-%02u %02u:%02u␀␀␀␀Modified␀␀␀␀Local date and time of the selected RASP file.␀␀Overlay opacity␀Sets the opacity of the RASP weather overlay on the map.  0% is fully transparent, 100% is fully opaque.␀␀␀␀Draws contour lines onto the RASP weather overlay.  Denser settings draw more lines; "Off" disables the contour lines.␀␀Page␀␀␀␀%s %u - %s:␀Layer␀␀␀RASP weather layer for the current map page. Use Apply to page to commit changes.␀␀␀Forecast time for the current map page. Opens the same picker as the weather controls (Auto, Now, or a fixed quarter-hour slot).␀␀␀␀Apply to page␀␀␀Add page␀␀␀␀Pages setup␀{:.0f}␀␀Wide␀␀␀␀Regular␀Fine␀␀␀␀RaspContours␀␀␀␀RaspLayerOpacity␀␀␀␀Third-party␀Credits␀The news of each version are on https://xcsoar.org␀␀98763f65b0␀␀![XCSoar Logo](resource:IDB_LOGO_HD)

